Author:     djules75 <1819336+djules75@users.noreply.github.com>
AuthorDate: Fri Oct 9 22:26:41 2026 +0800
Commit:     djules75 <1819336+djules75@users.noreply.github.com>
CommitDate: Sun Oct 11 09:27:52 2026 +0800

    History: wait for a busy slot instead of resetting continuity (V441)
    
    Issue 19 (Skyrim VR on a Steam Frame, SteamVR 72 Hz, D3D11 bridge, NVIDIA
    backend) drops to about 50 in episodes that grow until permanent. The logs
    show the prime-burst cycle of issue 16 on SteamVR: a capture finds its
    history slot still read by the GPU, continuity resets, the frames after the
    prime arrive bunched, the GPU falls ~50 ms behind and the next capture is
    busy again. 125 ms per cycle, 32 real and 16 generated images a second. On
    V440 a loading screen put it in the cycle and it never left.
    
    A capture that finds its slot busy on a GPU fence (the reader's completion
    or the slot's own capture) now waits for it, for at most one display
    period (11.1 ms before one is known, never more than 20 ms), then fails as
    before. A slot held by an unsubmitted lease is never waited for. The wait
    has its own event and a high-resolution timer, so a timed-out wait cannot
    wake the unbounded waits early and the limit is not rounded to the system
    tick. Measured in the issue's three NVIDIA logs, 98-100% of the busy
    captures would have found their slot free within one period, half within
    6-7 ms, against the 60 ms the failed frame stalled in xrEndFrame anyway.
    
    New record history_capture_wait for each capture that found its slot busy
    (result the capture's HRESULT, b microseconds waited, c limit << 32 |
    image); the summary script reports them. New history test covers a reader
    that finishes inside the limit, one that does not, a lease, and a free slot.
    
    ctest 87/87. Not run in a headset.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

